---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const highlights = [
	{
		tag: 'Core Claim',
		title: 'Anti-Crystallization Principle',
		copy:
			'Future-bearing systems persist only inside a nondegenerate interval between dissolution and total crystallization.',
	},
	{
		tag: 'Main Result',
		title: 'Crystallization Drift Theorem',
		copy:
			'The same self-reinforcing mechanisms that keep a system alive also push it toward organizational closure.',
	},
	{
		tag: 'Unification',
		title: 'Cross-domain reductions',
		copy:
			'The program derives dissipative structures, edge-of-chaos dynamics, free energy minimization, quantum Darwinism, information bounds, and Price/Fisher selection as special cases.',
	},
];

const architecture = [
	{
		title: 'Paper',
		copy:
			'The working manuscript states the ACP, proves the CDT, and packages the cross-domain story into a single theory spine.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Proofs',
		copy:
			'Standalone proof documents hold the core theorem chain, the coherent steering derivation, and the newer meta-theoretic coherence work.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Bridges',
		copy:
			'Bridge documents translate the theory into empirical predictions, Schur-complement structure, coordination floors, and arithmetic/operator regimes.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Reductions',
		copy:
			'Formal reductions show how familiar results from separate literatures instantiate the same structural law under domain-specific variables.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Essays',
		copy:
			'Companion essays provide the philosophical frame and help translate the theory for readers outside the formal proof track.',
	},
	{
		title: 'Open Problems',
		copy:
			'The project keeps a live tracker of what remains unsolved, especially around coherence crisis dynamics, operator bridges, and empirical test design.',
	},
];

const currentSignals = [
	{ value: 'v0.9', label: 'Current internal paper draft' },
	{ value: '10', label: 'Formal predictions with protocols' },
	{ value: '6+', label: 'Reduction families already integrated' },
];
---

<Layout
	title="Persistence Theory | A General Theory of Persistence"
	description="A first public-facing site for the Persistence Theory project, centered on the Anti-Crystallization Principle and the Crystallization Drift Theorem."
>
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="hero-copy">
			<span class="eyebrow">Research program</span>
			<h1>A general theory of persistence.</h1>
			<p>
				The Persistence Theory project asks a sharp structural question: what lets a
				system keep generating real futures without dissolving into noise or freezing
				into total determinism? The current answer is the
				<strong> Anti-Crystallization Principle</strong>, with the
				<strong> Crystallization Drift Theorem</strong> giving the project’s central
				novel result.
			</p>

			<div class="button-row">
				<a class="button primary" href="/theory">Read the theory map</a>
				<a class="button" href="/program">See the program status</a>
			</div>

			<div class="stat-grid">
				{
					currentSignals.map((signal) => (
						<div class="stat">
							<strong>{signal.value}</strong>
							<span>{signal.label}</span>
						</div>
					))
				}
			</div>
		</div>

		<aside class="hero-panel">
			<h2>What this site is for</h2>
			<p>
				This first version is a public layer for a markdown-first formalization
				workspace. It is meant to explain the project clearly, show where the theory
				stands, and give the website room to grow into a more polished publication
				surface later.
			</p>

			<div class="dense">
				<div>
					<strong>Current emphasis</strong>
					<p>Clarify the theory spine, map the research architecture, and expose the active fronts.</p>
				</div>
				<div>
					<strong>Recent additions</strong>
					<p>Restraint-Power / Heisenberg, Price-Fisher integration, non-Gaussian bounds, and meta-theoretic coherence.</p>
				</div>
				<div>
					<strong>Next site move</strong>
					<p>Import selected essays and paper sections into publishable public pages.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</aside>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="section-head">
			<span class="section-kicker">Core structure</span>
			<h2 class="section-title">The productive interval</h2>
			<p class="section-intro">
				The project’s central intuition is simple and severe: systems that remain
				capable of meaningful change must avoid two absorbing boundaries at once.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="interval-grid">
			<article class="interval-card">
				<h3>Dissolution</h3>
				<p class="subtle">
					Maximum entropy erases coherent structure. The system loses the ability to
					sustain a future-bearing trajectory.
				</p>
				<ul class="mini-list">
					<li>Thermodynamic equilibrium</li>
					<li>Loss of organized distinctions</li>
					<li>Death by dispersion</li>
				</ul>
			</article>

			<article class="interval-card featured">
				<h3>Persistence interval</h3>
				<p class="subtle">
					The live regime is neither random nor fully closed. It keeps enough order to
					cohere and enough openness to continue generating new macroscopic futures.
				</p>
				<ul class="mini-list">
					<li>Nonzero conditional macrostate entropy</li>
					<li>Stability without lock-in</li>
					<li>Persistence as active maintenance</li>
				</ul>
			</article>

			<article class="interval-card">
				<h3>Crystallization</h3>
				<p class="subtle">
					Total internal closure kills the future by removing real alternatives. The
					system remains, but its open-ended becoming does not.
				</p>
				<ul class="mini-list">
					<li>Zero conditional macrostate entropy</li>
					<li>Pathological lock-in and overfit</li>
					<li>Death by stasis</li>
				</ul>
			</article>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="section-head">
			<span class="section-kicker">What the program proves</span>
			<h2 class="section-title">One theory spine, several fronts</h2>
		</div>

		<div class="card-grid">
			{
				highlights.map((item) => (
					<article class="card">
						<span class="tag">{item.tag}</span>
						<h3>{item.title}</h3>
						<p>{item.copy}</p>
					</article>
				))
			}
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="pillars">
			<div class="callout">
				<strong>Why it matters</strong>
				<p>
					If the theory holds, the pressure toward rigidity is not an accidental flaw
					of successful systems. It is a structural consequence of the same mechanisms
					that protect them from collapse.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="quote">
				The mechanisms that prevent dissolution are the same mechanisms that drive a
				system toward crystallization.
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="section-head">
			<span class="section-kicker">Research architecture</span>
			<h2 class="section-title">The workspace is already shaped like a program</h2>
			<p class="section-intro">
				The formalization is organized as a living research environment rather than a
				single manuscript folder. That makes the site a natural next layer, not a
				separate project universe.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="card-grid">
			{
				architecture.map((item) => (
					<article class="card">
						<h3>{item.title}</h3>
						<p>{item.copy}</p>
					</article>
				))
			}
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="strip">
			<p>
				The next clean iteration is to turn selected essays, theorem summaries, and
				visual diagrams into native website content while keeping
				<code>Formalization/</code> as the source-of-truth research layer.
			</p>
			<a class="button primary" href="/program">Open the program map</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>
